"The patient underwent a robot-assisted radical prostatectomy with a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) and pathological stage pT2c. Favorable findings include negative surgical margins, no seminal vesicle invasion, absence of lymphovascular invasion, and no lymph node metastasis. Preoperative PSA was 5.9. These positive surgical findings suggest a favorable short-term prognosis. Countervailing factors include the preoperative PSA level and the missing biopsy grade/ISUP report, which introduces uncertainty regarding the true tumor characteristics. The nodal status was documented as negative. The prognosis is influenced by the postoperative PSA trajectory; missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly widens the uncertainty regarding future disease control. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory is crucial for refining this assessment. The fixed estimated horizon is 59.10 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 44.32–73.87 months; its coverage has not been validated."
